name: state machines
intro: Many programs are really one idea: "what am I in the middle of?" A
intro: tokenizer is in the middle of a number, or a string. A protocol parser
intro: is waiting for a header, or reading a body. That answer is the state,
intro: and the whole program is: read one thing, look at (state, input),
intro: act, pick the next state.
intro:
intro: Write the states as an enum, loop over the input, switch on the state:
intro:
intro:     enum state { START, IN_NUM, IN_STR };
intro:
intro:     enum state s = START;
intro:     int c;
intro:     while ((c = getchar()) != EOF) {
intro:         switch (s) {
intro:         case START:
intro:             if (isdigit(c)) s = IN_NUM;
intro:             break;
intro:         case IN_NUM:
intro:             if (!isdigit(c)) s = START;
intro:             break;
intro:         ...
intro:         }
intro:     }
intro:
intro: Each `case` answers one question: given this state and this byte, what
intro: now? Keep that to a few lines and the machine stays readable. Do not
intro: keep "am I in a string" in three booleans -- the booleans can disagree
intro: (string and comment at once). One enum cannot.
intro:
intro: When the next state depends only on (state, input) and nothing else
intro: happens, a table says it shorter: `next[state][input]`. Use a table
intro: when every cell is data; use a switch when states do different work.
intro:
intro: A byte that ends one token often starts the next (the space after a
intro: number is fine, the `+` after it is not). Give it back with
intro: `ungetc(c, stdin)` and the START state sees it again. One byte of
intro: pushback is guaranteed.
intro:
intro: At EOF the loop ends, but the machine may be mid-token. Always ask
intro: what each state means at end of input: a number just ends, a string
intro: is an error. Ignoring this is how real parsers accept half a file.
---
q: div3.c -- is this binary number a multiple of 3
spec: Read characters from standard input. Each `0` or `1` is the next bit of
spec: a binary number, most significant first. Print `yes` if the number is
spec: divisible by 3, otherwise `no`, then exit 0. Empty input is the number
spec: 0, so it prints `yes`. Newlines are skipped.
spec:
spec: Any other character prints `div3: bad character` to standard error and
spec: exits 2. The input can be any length, so do not build the number: keep
spec: only its remainder mod 3 as the state. Reading a bit b turns n into
spec: 2n + b.
file: div3.c
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: 
prog: /* state = the remainder of the number so far, divided by 3.
prog:    reading a bit b turns n into 2n + b, so the new remainder
prog:    depends only on the old remainder and b. */
prog: static const int next[3][2] = {
prog: 	{0, 1},
prog: 	{2, 0},
prog: 	{1, 2},
prog: };
prog: 
prog: int main(void)
prog: {
prog: 	int state = 0;
prog: 	int c;
prog: 	while ((c = getchar()) != EOF) {
prog: 		if (c == '\n')
prog: 			continue;
prog: 		if (c != '0' && c != '1') {
prog: 			fprintf(stderr, "div3: bad character\n");
prog: 			return 2;
prog: 		}
prog: 		state = next[state][c - '0'];
prog: 	}
prog: 	puts(state == 0 ? "yes" : "no");
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: printf '110' | div3
case: printf '1001\n' | div3
case: printf '111' | div3
case: printf '' | div3
case: awk 'BEGIN { for (i = 0; i < 70; i++) print "1" }' | tr -d '\n' | div3; awk 'BEGIN { for (i = 0; i < 71; i++) print "1" }' | tr -d '\n' | div3
case: printf '10x1' | div3 2>&1; echo $?
hint: the state is 0, 1 or 2; from remainder r, reading bit b gives (2*r + b) % 3 -- a table of three rows and two columns will do
